Given,

\[
\vec{w}(t)= e^t \vec{u}_x + e^{-2t}\vec{u}_y
\]

Since \(\vec{u}_x\) and \(\vec{u}_y\) are unit vectors along \(x\) and \(y\) axes,

\[
\vec{w}(t)=
\begin{bmatrix}
e^t\\
e^{-2t}
\end{bmatrix}
\]

Differentiate with respect to \(t\):

\[
\dot{\vec{w}}(t)=
\begin{bmatrix}
e^t\\
-2e^{-2t}
\end{bmatrix}
\]

The given differential equation is

\[
\dot{\vec{w}} = A\vec{w}
\]

So,

\[
A
\begin{bmatrix}
e^t\\
e^{-2t}
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
e^t\\
-2e^{-2t}
\end{bmatrix}
\]

This is satisfied by

\[
A=
\begin{bmatrix}
1 & 0\\
0 & -2
\end{bmatrix}
\]

This corresponds to option (A).

FINAL_ANSWER: A

For the given two-port network:

- Left series impedance: \(Z_1 = 1\,k\Omega = 1000\,\Omega\)
- Shunt impedance: \(1\,k\Omega\), so admittance  
  \[
  Y = \frac{1}{1000} = 10^{-3}\,\Omega^{-1}
  \]
- Right series impedance: \(Z_2 = 1\,k\Omega = 1000\,\Omega\)

Using ABCD matrices:

For a series impedance \(Z\):

\[
\begin{bmatrix}
1 & Z \\
0 & 1
\end{bmatrix}
\]

For a shunt admittance \(Y\):

\[
\begin{bmatrix}
1 & 0 \\
Y & 1
\end{bmatrix}
\]

So the overall ABCD matrix is:

\[
\begin{bmatrix}
1 & 1000 \\
0 & 1
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
1 & 0 \\
10^{-3} & 1
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
1 & 1000 \\
0 & 1
\end{bmatrix}
\]

First multiply the first two matrices:

\[
\begin{bmatrix}
1 & 1000 \\
0 & 1
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
1 & 0 \\
10^{-3} & 1
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
2 & 1000 \\
10^{-3} & 1
\end{bmatrix}
\]

Now multiply with the last matrix:

\[
\begin{bmatrix}
2 & 1000 \\
10^{-3} & 1
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
1 & 1000 \\
0 & 1
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
2 & 3000 \\
10^{-3} & 2
\end{bmatrix}
\]

Thus,

\[
A = 2,\quad B = 3 \times 10^3\,\Omega,\quad C = 10^{-3}\,\Omega^{-1},\quad D = 2
\]

FINAL_ANSWER: D
